  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "The equality \\(\\dfrac1{x-1}=\\dfrac2{x-2}\\) is satisfied by:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "no real values of \\(x\\)",
    "B": "either \\(x=1\\) or \\(x=2\\)",
    "C": "only \\(x=1\\)",
    "D": "only \\(x=2\\)",
    "E": "only \\(x=0\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "First, the denominators require \\(x\\ne1\\) and \\(x\\ne2\\). For every other real value, we may multiply both sides by the nonzero product \\((x-1)(x-2)\\). This gives\n\\[\nx-2=2(x-1)=2x-2,\n\\]\nso \\(x=0\\).\n\nThis value is allowed, and substitution into the original equation gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{0-1}=-1=\\frac2{0-2}.\n\\]\nAll the transformations were reversible on the allowed domain, so there are no other solutions. Thus only \\(\\boxed{x=0}\\) satisfies the equality, choice \\(\\text{E}\\).",
